    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and both tests pass (`pytest -q`: the existing status test and a new `tests/test_claude.py`).\n\n**Why it doesn't just reuse `build_http_client()`:** the pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` is built on `httpx2`, not `httpx`. It raises an error if you give it an `httpx.Client` or an `httpx` transport, so the client from `app/http.py` can't be passed in. Instead, `build_claude` creates the SDK's own `httpx2`-based client and applies the same settings from `app/http.py`:\n- **Header:** `X-Egress-Tag` is set to `EGRESS_TAG`, on the HTTP client and as the SDK's default header.\n- **Logging:** `log_egress` is registered as the request hook. The SDK sends every retry through that client, so each attempt is logged once on `app.egress`.\n- **Timeouts:** built from `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` (5s) and `READ_TIMEOUT` (60s). I pass them to the SDK directly as well, because otherwise it can ignore the client's timeout and use its own default.\n- `max_retries=2`.\n- When `transport` is given, the client sends through it. When it isn't, I leave it out entirely rather than passing `None`, so the SDK keeps its default proxy and keepalive setup.\n\n`app/http.py`, `fetch_status` and the existing test are unchanged.\n\n**What this means for callers:** a transport passed to `build_claude` must be an `httpx2` transport such as `httpx2.MockTransport`. An `httpx.MockTransport` will be rejected. `httpx2` comes with `anthropic`, so no new dependency was added.\n\n**The new test** uses a stub transport that returns a 500 and then a 200. It checks:\n- the answer comes back after the retry;\n- both attempts carry the header;\n- `app.egress` logs exactly two `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` lines;\n- requests go out with a 5s connect and 60s read timeout."
  },
